# Claude Code Plugin @claude-code-plugin
{
    @meta {
        type: doc

        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    @about {
        The SDOC plugin for Claude Code: what it adds, how to install it for yourself or roll it out to a team, and how it is built and tested. Read it before installing the plugin, when deciding how to distribute it, or before changing anything under `plugins/sdoc-lexica/`.
    }

    @reading-guide {
        {[table]
            Sections | Note
            @what-it-does, @install | Installing it for yourself
            @team-rollout, @updates | Rolling it out to a team
            @plugin-development | Changing the plugin
        }
    }

    # What It Does @what-it-does
    {
        The plugin teaches Claude Code to read, write and navigate SDOC files and Lexica knowledge trees, the `lexica/` directories a project keeps its knowledge in. It loads knowledge a section at a time instead of a file at a time, and it puts each file's signposts in front of Claude at the moment they apply.

        It runs inside Claude Code as a **mod**: a hooks module Claude Code executes in its own runtime. No Node.js, Python or other runtime is needed on the machine.

        # Skills @plugin-skills
        {
            {[table]
                Skill | What it covers
                `/sdoc-lexica:authoring` | The syntax, the mistakes that break a file, signposts, and reading a large file by section. The full authoring guide ships with it, to be read by section.
                `/sdoc-lexica:lexica` | Finding knowledge in `lexica/` directories, keeping it current (impl-status, specs, conventions), and starting a new knowledge file.
            }

            Claude loads a skill when the task calls for it, and either can be invoked by name.
        }

        # Tools @plugin-tools
        {
            Read-only tools Claude calls directly. In a project with a `lexica/` directory they are always listed; elsewhere they wait behind tool search and cost nothing until wanted. They read only inside the project, and the plugin's own bundled guide, with no permission prompt. A path elsewhere, a link pointing out of the project included, is refused: Claude uses Read for that.

            {[table]
                Tool | Answers
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__list` | Every `.sdoc` file under the `lexica/` directories, or the directories given, each with its type, size and about summary
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__outline` | A file's sections: id, title, line range and first words, plus the signposts it has
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__read` | Sections by id, as source with line numbers, ready to edit from
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__search` | The sections that hold every word of a query
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__guides` | A file's about, reading guide, related resources and editing guide
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__check` | Parse errors and signpost, reference and citation findings, as `build-doc --check` reports them
            }
        }

        # Hooks @plugin-hooks
        {
            {[table]
                When | What Claude is given
                The first prompt of a conversation, in a project with `lexica/` | An index of the knowledge files with their about summaries, and how to load them by section, within the `indexSize` setting. A summary is never cut short: when they do not all fit, every file is listed by its title instead, and only if even that does not fit are files left out, with a count. Sent again after compaction or a clear.
                The first time Claude opens an `.sdoc` file, by Read or by the outline, read or guides tool | The file's reading guide and related resources, when it has them
                A Read of a file over 300 lines, whole | The file's outline, to read by section next time
                The first edit of a file with an editing guide | The edit is refused once, with the guide; made again, it goes through
                Every edit of an `.sdoc` file | The findings the edit added (a parse error, a broken reference, a signpost of the wrong shape), and nothing about what was already there
            }

            Each happens once per conversation and file, and once per agent: a subagent gets its own. In a project with no `.sdoc` files the plugin adds nothing.
        }
    }

    # Settings @plugin-settings
    {
        Set them with `/plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc` in a session, or from a shell by piping JSON to `claude plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc --values-stdin`. A change applies to sessions started after it.

        {[table]
            Setting | Values | What it does
            `indexSize` | `6k`, `12k` (default), `24k` | The most the knowledge index adds to a conversation's first prompt, in characters: about 1,500, 3,000 or 6,000 tokens. How many files fit with their summaries depends on how long the summaries are: at 12k, about 40 files whose summaries are two or three sentences, and about half that with longer ones.
            `verbose` | `false` (default), `true` | Shows each thing the plugin does as a line in the session (@plugin-verbose).
        }
    }

    # Seeing It Work @plugin-verbose
    {
        What the plugin hands Claude, such as the index, a reading guide or an edit's findings, is never shown in the session. Two ways to see it work:

        {[.]
            - **The verbose option.** Each thing the plugin does is shown as one dim line in the session, which Claude is not sent: the index it attached, what it added after a Read and why it added nothing, an edit it refused, the findings it reported, each tool call. Turn it on with `/plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc` in a session, or from a shell, then start a new session:
            {
                ```
echo '{"verbose":"true"}' | claude plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc --values-stdin
                ```
            }
            - **The debug log.** The same lines are always written there, verbose or not, together with the engine's own: the module loading, each tool registered, each hook's duration, and any hook skipped and why.
            {
                ```
claude --debug-file ~/sdoc-debug.log
tail -f ~/sdoc-debug.log | grep --line-buffered sdoc-lexica
                ```
            }
        }

        `claude plugin details` reports no hooks and no tools for this plugin. It does not count what a mod sets up when a session starts; the plugin is not missing anything.
    }

    # Installing It @install
    {
        In a Claude Code session:

        ```
/plugin install sdoc-lexica --marketplace entropicwarrior/sdoc
        ```

        Answer `y` to add the marketplace, then pick a scope: user, for every project. From a shell, the same is `claude plugin install sdoc-lexica --marketplace entropicwarrior/sdoc`.

        The repository holds the whole SDOC project. To fetch only what the plugin needs, add the marketplace with a sparse checkout first:

        ```
claude plugin marketplace add entropicwarrior/sdoc --sparse .claude-plugin plugins
claude plugin install sdoc-lexica@sdoc
        ```

        # Requirements @plugin-requirements
        {
            {[.]
                - A recent Claude Code. The plugin was built and tested on 2.1.311.
                - Mods enabled. An organisation's managed settings can switch them off. The skills still load then, but the tools and hooks do not.
            }

            The mod interface is in early access and can change between Claude Code releases. When an update breaks the plugin, `claude plugin validate` on its folder names what changed.
        }
    }

    # Rolling It Out to a Team @team-rollout
    {
        Two settings make Claude Code offer the plugin to everyone: the marketplace, and the plugin enabled from it. Pin the marketplace to `main`, which carries releases only; the default branch, `develop`, moves between them.

        ```json
{
  "extraKnownMarketplaces": {
    "sdoc": {
      "source": { "source": "github", "repo": "entropicwarrior/sdoc", "ref": "main" },
      "autoUpdate": true
    }
  },
  "enabledPlugins": { "sdoc-lexica@sdoc": true }
}
        ```

        {[.]
            - **For a whole organisation:** an administrator puts this in Claude Code's managed settings (in the claude.ai admin console, or the `managed-settings.json` a device manager deploys). Every member gets the plugin, and cannot turn its marketplace's auto-update off.
            - **For one repository:** commit it as `.claude/settings.json`. Each person who opens the repository and trusts the folder gets the plugin for that project.
        }
    }

    # Updates @updates
    {
        The plugin sets no version number, so Claude Code versions it by commit. Every commit on the branch you track is an update, and on `main` that means every release and every plugin fix merged there. With auto-update on, Claude Code fetches it in the background and asks for `/reload-plugins`. Without it, run `claude plugin update sdoc-lexica@sdoc`, then `/reload-plugins` in a running session.

        `/plugin` therefore shows a commit rather than a version number, and an SDOC release that leaves the plugin unchanged still counts as an update.
    }

    # Changing the Plugin @plugin-development
    {
        # Layout @plugin-layout
        {
            {[table]
                Path | Holds
                `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` | The marketplace, listing `plugins/sdoc-lexica`
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/hooks/register.mjs` | The hooks module: binds Claude Code's events to the handlers
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/hooks/handlers.mjs` | Everything the plugin does, written against a small file-access interface so Node tests can drive it
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/skills/` | The two skills
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/lib/` | Generated ES-module copies of `src/sdoc.js` and `src/knowledge-base.js`
                `src/knowledge-base.js` | Discovery, outlines, section reads, search, signposts and checks over SDOC text; runtime-neutral, and shared with `tools/build-doc.js`
            }
        }

        # Rules @plugin-rules
        {
            {[.]
                - **Nothing the hooks module loads may use Node.** No `require`, `fs`, `path` or `process`: Claude Code runs the module without them. Files are reached through `$.fs`, in `register.mjs` only.
                - **Edit the sources, never `plugins/sdoc-lexica/lib/`.** Run `npm run build:plugin` after changing `src/sdoc.js`, `src/knowledge-base.js` or the authoring guide, and commit what it writes; a marketplace install runs no build step. The tests fail while a generated file is stale.
                - **Never give the plugin a version.** Not in `plugin.json`, and not in the marketplace entry. Once a version is set, Claude Code updates the plugin only when that number changes, so a fix shipped without a bump reaches nobody, and nothing says so. Without one, every commit is a version. `claude plugin validate` warns that no version is set; that warning is expected.
                - **The index goes with the first prompt, not in the first message's context.** Claude Code's built-in security plugin passes over a user plugin's hook on that event.
                - **A tool call the plugin answers skips Claude Code's permission check.** That check runs beneath the step the plugin's hook does not take. The hook keeps its own guard: every path, and where it leads through links, inside the project or the plugin. Keep any new tool behind it.
                - **A skill is named after its folder.** `skills/lexica/` is `/sdoc-lexica:lexica`; a `name:` in the frontmatter does not change it, so the two must agree.
            }
        }

        # Shipping a Fix @plugin-hotfix
        {
            The mod interface is in early access, so a Claude Code update can break the plugin with no SDOC change at all. A fix for that does not wait for a release:

            {[#]
                1. Branch `hotfix/plugin-<what>` from `main`.
                2. Fix it, run `npm test`, and check it by hand as @plugin-testing describes.
                3. Open a pull request to `main`. Once it is merged, coworkers tracking `main` have the fix with their next update.
                4. Merge `main` back into `develop`.
            }

            Publish no GitHub release for it. The npm and VS Code Marketplace publishing runs on a release, and nothing outside the plugin changed.
        }

        # Testing @plugin-testing
        {
            `npm test` runs `test/test-plugin.js`, which covers:

            {[.]
                - the generated files and manifests;
                - that the ES-module parser builds the same tree as `src/sdoc.js` for every file in the repository;
                - the knowledge-base functions;
                - every hook and tool through an in-memory file system.
            }

            The tests cannot show the engine calling the hooks. Before a release that changes the plugin, check it by hand:

            ```
claude plugin validate plugins/sdoc-lexica
claude plugin validate .
claude --plugin-dir plugins/sdoc-lexica
            ```

            In that session, in a project with a `lexica/` directory:

            {[.]
                - ask what knowledge index Claude was given;
                - Read a file with a reading guide;
                - edit a file with an editing guide (refused once, then applied);
                - break a reference in an edit and look for the finding;
                - call a tool on a file in the project (answered, with no prompt) and on one outside it (refused).
                - with `verbose` on, watch a line appear for each of these.
            }

            `claude --debug-file <path>` logs every hook the engine ran, skipped or refused.
        }
    }
}
